        q: 'What is the difference between at-least-once, at-most-once, and exactly-once delivery in Kafka?',
        a: 'At-most-once: commit offset before processing — messages may be lost on failure. At-least-once: commit after processing — messages may be processed twice (most common, handle by making consumers idempotent). Exactly-once: use Kafka transactions (enable.idempotence=true + transactional.id) — strongest guarantee, higher overhead.',
        detailedAnswer: 'At-least-once is the practical default for most pipelines: set `enable.auto.commit=false`, process the batch, then manually commit with `consumer.commitSync()`. If the process crashes after processing but before commit, the same messages are reprocessed — design your sink to handle duplicates (upsert by event ID). Exactly-once in Kafka Streams or within Kafka → Kafka is achievable. Exactly-once into external systems (e.g., Kafka → PostgreSQL) requires idempotent sinks or two-phase commit.',
